     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p>Does it mean that the data-fitted model is reliable if p are very small such as &lt;0.05? </p><p>Is the smaller the P value the better data-fitted model?</p><p>OR just inverse?<br></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>As in any other null-hypothesis significance testing context, smaller p values than your alpha level imply you should reject the null hypothesis.  The null hypothesis is that your data are consistent with your model.  If very few replications produce worse fit than your observed data, then the degree to which your model fails to reproduce your data is uncommonly poor.</div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422786729000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1JgI0Ql17SMYYiay8Fla2m\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div><div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
